<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Android Lint severities for every module (wired in from build-logic's `configureKotlinAndroid`,
  so there is exactly one of these files and no module can quietly hold a different opinion).

  The project runs lint with `abortOnError = true`, which means *this file decides what fails the
  build*: an issue at `error` (or `fatal`) stops it, an issue at `warning` or below is reported and
  ignored. The split it draws is deliberate — accessibility regressions fail, the rest is advisory
  (accessibility audit 2026-08-05, CR-7 / A11Y-LINT-01).
-->
<lint>

    <!--
      ## The gate: accessibility findings are errors.

      These are the checks a regression in this area would trip. They are all View/XML-shaped,
      which in a 100%-Compose app means they normally find nothing — that is the point. They are a
      tripwire for the day someone adds an XML layout, an `AndroidView` with a touch listener, or a
      `MediaRouteButton`-style platform widget, and reaches for the shortcut that leaves it
      unlabelled or unreachable. What they cannot see — Compose semantics, merged card nodes, live
      regions, traversal order — is held by the instrumented suite instead
      (`*/src/androidTest`, run with `connectedDebugAndroidTest`).
    -->
    <!-- An ImageView/ImageButton with no contentDescription — the oldest a11y bug there is. -->
    <issue id="ContentDescription" severity="error" />
    <!-- setOnTouchListener without performClick: a control no accessibility service can press. -->
    <issue id="ClickableViewAccessibility" severity="error" />
    <!--
      Touch target size is *not* here. `TouchTargetSizeCheck` is an Accessibility Test Framework
      check, not an Android Lint issue — lint rejects the id outright (and `UnknownIssueId` below
      makes that a build failure rather than a silent no-op). ATF runs it against the live Compose
      semantics tree in the instrumented suite, which is also the only place it could see a
      `Modifier.size()` anyway. See `core/ui/src/androidTest`.
    -->
    <!-- android:clickable without android:focusable — unreachable by keyboard or d-pad. -->
    <issue id="KeyboardInaccessibleWidget" severity="error" />
    <!-- An EditText with no labelFor: a field that announces its value and nothing else (CR-2). -->
    <issue id="LabelFor" severity="error" />

    <!--
      A typo in an id above would silently disable the check it was meant to switch on, so an
      unrecognised id is itself a build failure.
    -->
    <issue id="UnknownIssueId" severity="error" />

    <!--
      ## Advisory: the families that already fail today, demoted so the gate stays a11y-shaped.

      Before this file, lint ran with `abortOnError = false` and *nothing* it found could fail the
      build. Turning that on to make the accessibility checks bite would also have made these 293
      pre-existing findings fatal, which is a different project's worth of work. Demoting them to
      `warning` leaves their enforcement exactly where it has always been — visible in the report,
      not blocking — while everything else lint calls an error now blocks.

      A baseline file was the alternative and was rejected: it would freeze 293 findings into a
      generated XML blob that has to be regenerated every time a string is added, and it would hide
      them from the report rather than leave them in it.

      Each entry below is a real thing to fix one day, with its count at the time of writing.
    -->
    <!-- 200×. The 69 translated locales are missing plural quantities English does not have. -->
    <issue id="MissingQuantity" severity="warning" />
    <!-- 40×. Same corpus: a 'one' bucket that covers 0 and 1 without a %d in the string. -->
    <issue id="ImpliedQuantity" severity="warning" />
    <!--
      45×. The strings the accessibility waves added (2026-08-05) exist in English only; the rest
      of the app is translated into all 69 locales. Tracked as follow-up work, not as a11y work.
    -->
    <issue id="MissingTranslation" severity="warning" />
    <!-- 7×. Media3's @UnstableApi surface, used deliberately in the DI modules and the player. -->
    <issue id="UnsafeOptInUsageError" severity="warning" />

    <!--
      The one pre-existing error that was fixed rather than demoted: `NonObservableLocale` in
      `PlayerControls.TagPill`. Its id could not be listed here in any case — it ships with the
      Compose lint checks, so the modules that have no Compose on their classpath would have
      reported it as an unknown id, which this file makes fatal.
    -->

</lint>
